{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff -- src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md | sed -n '1,280p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff -- src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md | sed -n '1,280p'\"","aggregated_output":"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-GuY5Tiqf' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-01 22:47:18.697 xcodebuild[97727:37323603]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-01 22:47:19.031 xcodebuild[97727:37323600] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ilJXw4M2' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-01 22:47:19.737 xcodebuild[97733:37323642]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-01 22:47:19.938 xcodebuild[97733:37323641] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md b/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\nindex 78016fa4..31659eb2 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\n@@ -120,6 +120,143 @@ definition a remote client makes is visible at the local prompt on the next\n tick. The endpoint evaluates locally or reports that it does not proxy.\n `--host 127.0.0.1` remains the default boundary.\n \n+## Publishing and loading code over the DHT\n+\n+With `--index-store dht:<dir>` the code index is the durable, exclusively\n+locked directory store with a `dao.space.dht` node composed over it: the\n+node joins the DHT, shares the store's content when told to, and fetches\n+what a `require` asks for and the node does not hold. The `--dht-*` flags\n+are refused with any other store.\n+\n+**The flags.** `--dht-peer host:port` (repeatable) names the bootstrap\n+contacts; the host must be an IP literal, and no peer means a solo node\n+that opens no socket, whose publications stay durable in the directory\n+and acknowledged by no one. `--dht-publish` shares everything the\n+directory holds with any peer that asks for an address; without it the\n+node fetches only. `--dht-bind ip` and `--dht-port p` set the socket's\n+address, loopback and ephemeral unless given, and need a peer;\n+`--dht-max-inbound-bytes n` bounds the payload the node accepts from\n+peers, 64 MiB by default. `--dht-manifest :segment/...`, with or without\n+its leading colon, hands the node a remote index manifest to hydrate\n+before the first evaluation, and needs a peer too. `--dht-key file` loads\n+the publisher's stable Ed25519 key file, `--dht-keygen file` writes one\n+and exits, and `--dht-principal hex` (repeatable, 64 lowercase\n+hexadecimal digits) declares a publisher whose signed names this node\n+honors. A bind that fails, or a hydration that cannot complete, refuses\n+the shell with exit status 1 instead of composing over an empty index.\n+\n+```bash\n+clj -M:clj-yin-repl --index-store dht:/tmp/yin-a --dht-peer 127.0.0.1:4001 \\\n+    --dht-publish --dht-key /tmp/yin-a.key\n+```\n+\n+The banner states, before the node steps once and so before anything is\n+shared, what will be shared: that publishing is on and everything in the\n+directory's content path will be shared, or that the node fetches only.\n+With `--dht-key` it also names the principal that signs every name\n+published here (`dht: names published here are signed by principal\n+ed25519:... (key from <file>)`); without one, that `(yin.link/publish ...)`\n+is refused while names are still read, resolved, loaded and linked. The\n+node's first ticks print where the socket bound (`dht: node ...\n+listening on 127.0.0.1:53812; peers: ...; publishing`, or `fetch-only`).\n+\n+**Publishing a module by signed name.** `(require (quote yin.link))`\n+installs the host module, and then `(yin.link/publish (quote my.lib)\n+(quote [f]))` derives the module from this session's indexed code: the\n+defining programs of the exports and, to a fixed point, of the free names\n+they read, minted into all four code formats under one schema-1 manifest\n+and materialized into the index store content-addressed. The answer names\n+the module, its manifest address, and the linker's own verdict per\n+format:\n+\n+```clojure\n+yin> (yin.link/publish (quote my.lib) (quote [f]))\n+{:module 'my.lib, :address :segment/..., :links {...}}\n+```\n+\n+The name binding is a set of Ed25519-signed envelopes binding the name to\n+that manifest address, committed through the session's indexer as one\n+transaction whose HEAD write announces the publication. The node then\n+reports the result: `dht: published :segment/... (N blobs)` followed by\n+`acknowledged: sent to N peers`, or a PARTIAL or NOT acknowledged line\n+naming the count of blobs not sent, the first reason, and whether it is\n+retrying; a publication that is not acknowledged is retried in the\n+background while the node is open. Republishing a name at another\n+manifest retracts the standing assertion first. `:links` is per format,\n+and a format it refused at publish time (an export reading a module-level\n+definition from inside a lambda refuses the tree format) refuses at\n+require time too.\n+\n+**Loading it in another yin.repl.** Start the reader against a peer, with\n+the publisher's index manifest and its principal:\n+\n+```bash\n+clj -M:clj-yin-repl --index-store dht:/tmp/yin-b --dht-peer 127.0.0.1:53812 \\\n+    --dht-manifest :segment/... --dht-principal ed25519:...\n+```\n+\n+Hydration runs before the first evaluation: typed lines wait in the input\n+medium until the reader prints `dht: hydrated :<manifest>`, with the\n+datom and blob counts, and `evaluation admitted`. Then require by name:\n+\n+```clojure\n+yin> (require (quote my.lib))\n+;; require pending: my.lib (link N); lines typed meanwhile ...\n+yin> 'my.lib\n+yin> (my.lib/f 1)\n+4201\n+```\n+\n+A require whose closure the node does not hold prints the\n+`;; require pending: ...` line and parks: the node fetches the module's\n+closure from the peers, the parked require is re-checked when the load\n+ends, lines typed meanwhile run when it completes, and `(abandon)` gives\n+it up. The require answers the module's name and the export evaluates.\n+Each VM's session links the module itself, so after `(vm :type)` require\n+the name again: the same published name evaluates on all four VMs.\n+`(yin.link/names)` answers the names this node resolves, each with its\n+manifest address and the principal that asserted it.\n+\n+**Why a required name can be trusted.** Content is content-addressed,\n+and every hop re-verifies it: a blob fetched from a peer is checked\n+against the address it was asked for before it enters the local store,\n+and the closure walk re-checks the manifest, every tree row, every\n+derivation record and every image against its own address. A manifest\n+links at a name only if it declares that name. Names resolve only from\n+signed assertions of declared principals: an undeclared publisher, or a\n+proof that does not verify, refuses the require `:absent` with the\n+reasons as data. The same address asserted by several declared\n+principals resolves with every asserter named; two distinct addresses\n+refuse `:ambiguous-name`. A failed load is refused and forgotten, so a\n+later require starts a new load. The link source reads only the node's\n+own store, and the directory's lock keeps one owner per directory. A\n+retraction whose target no loaded snapshot holds is reported once,\n+globally, by `(yin.link/names)`, never as a per-name diagnostic.\n+`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md` is the contract for all of it.\n+\n+**The plain-Clojure path.** A non-REPL client takes the same path through\n+plain functions: `dao.space.dht/join` over a store, `load-index` for a\n+manifest address (step the node with `dao.space.dht/step`; `load-status`\n+answers `:loading`, `:loaded`, or `:failed` with the reason as data),\n+`yin.vm.linker.dht/names` to resolve names under an authority of\n+declared principals (`yin.vm.linker.dht/authority`),\n+`yin.vm.linker.dht/load-module` for a module's closure, and\n+`yin.vm.linker.dht/link` for one format's image; every failure answers\n+the data of that design's section 9. At the prompt,\n+`(require (quote dao.space.dht))` exposes the same calls as host\n+functions over the shell's own node.\n+\n+**Operational notes.** Keep the key file: a lost key can never sign\n+again, and a new key is a new principal. Receivers verify names against\n+the publisher's principal, not against where the bytes came from, so\n+hand the principal out with the network address and replace the\n+declaration when a key rotates. The Node reader the process tests spawn\n+is a build artifact: `bb build:yin-repl-node` writes `target/yin-repl.js`,\n+and a missing build fails `test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`. A fresh\n+worktree must run `clj -M:antlr-gen` before JVM test runs: the generated\n+parser path, `build/antlr/python3/classes`, is on the classpath and\n+absent until generated.\n+\n See [`docs/design/yin.repl.implementation-plan.md`](../../../../docs/design/yin.repl.implementation-plan.md)\n for the plan this REPL implements and the contract documents it is subordinate\n to.\n     1\t# Yin REPL Usage Guide\n     2\t\n     3\tThe DaoStream Yin REPL: a local shell, a `connect` that reaches a remote\n     4\tone over WebSockets, a `serve!` that answers, and nothing else. It runs on\n     5\t`yin.vm` and requires no legacy namespaces.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Starting and connecting\n     8\t\n     9\tThe entry points are the REPL aliases:\n    10\t\n    11\t```bash\n    12\tclj -M:clj-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n    13\t```\n    14\t\n    15\tFor ClojureDart (`cljd`), use the `cljd-yin-repl-build` alias to compile the Dart source, and then run the generated executable natively via `dart run`:\n    16\t\n    17\t```bash\n    18\tclj -M:cljd-yin-repl-build compile\n    19\tdart run bin/yin_repl_main.dart --port 8080 --headless\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\tFor ClojureScript (`cljs` on Node), you can run it directly using the shadow-cljs alias:\n    23\t\n    24\t```bash\n    25\tclj -M:cljs-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n    26\t```\n    27\t\n    28\tOr you can compile it to a standalone Node script:\n    29\t\n    30\t```bash\n    31\tnpx shadow-cljs release yin-repl\n    32\tnode target/yin-repl.js --port 8080 --headless\n    33\t```\n    34\t\n    35\t```clojure\n    36\tyin> (connect \"daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\")\n    37\tAttaching to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\n    38\tConnected to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\tAn absent URL path means `/repl`; an explicit `/` remains `/`.\n    42\t\n    43\t## Protocol and Architecture\n    44\t\n    45\t**`connect` returns immediately and reports its outcome when known.**\n    46\t`connect` composes the client boundary, attaches, and answers at once with\n    47\t`Attaching to …`; the driver prints `Connected to …` when the boundary\n    48\treports `/established`, and reports `/not-found` (an authoritative disclaimer,\n    49\tnot retried) or a reachability failure (which may succeed on retry) when that\n    50\tis what happened. No evaluation blocks on a promise, on any host.\n    51\t\n    52\t**`(vm :type)` offers four evaluators; the default is `:semantic`.**\n    53\tThe shell supports `:ast-walker` (the tree walker), `:semantic` (the linear\n    54\tsemantic VM), `:stack` (the de Bruijn stack kernel), and `:register` (the de\n    55\tBruijn register kernel). `:semantic` is the default when no `:vm-type` is\n    56\tgiven. Asking for any other type is an error naming what is supported.\n    57\tSwitching rebuilds the session and clears the value history.\n    58\t\n    59\tWhichever VM runs, the shell never reads a value from the VM record: a\n    60\thalted evaluation's value is appended to the output `dao.stream` medium as\n    61\ta `:repl/result` token, after the round's prints, and the shell reads it\n    62\tfrom there exactly as it reads printed output.\n    63\t\n    64\t**There is no `(telemetry)` command.** Telemetry is not part of this\n    65\tREPL architecture in any form. `(telemetry)` is answered with an unsupported\n    66\tnotice, and `--telemetry` / `--telemetry-stream` are rejected rather than\n    67\tignored. There is no `ws://` telemetry sink.\n    68\t\n    69\t**Evaluation runs on session-owned program media.** The REPL session\n    70\t(`make-session`), not any one VM, owns the program media for all four VMs.\n    71\tEvery program, including a datom program typed at the prompt, is appended\n    72\tto the session's ingress ring buffer (declared capacity 4096); the expander\n    73\tforwards it to the session's program medium, from which the selected VM\n    74\tingests it between batches. A gap (batches evicted before the VM saw them)\n    75\tis fatal to the current evaluation:\n    76\tthe loss is reported and the shell refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`,\n    77\trather than resuming as if execution were complete.\n    78\t\n    79\t**The evaluator is step-driven everywhere.** One `repl-step` owns all REPL and\n    80\tRPC state on every host; input adapters only append lines to the composition's\n    81\tinput medium. A remote evaluation returns immediately with a request id, and\n    82\tthe result prints when it completes. Input typed while a request is\n    83\toutstanding is queued, not evaluated; local control commands (`disconnect`,\n    84\t`quit`, `help`, `repl-state`) bypass that queue so a slow remote cannot trap\n    85\tthe operator.\n    86\t\n    87\t**A killed connection is reported, never timed out.** Losing the connection\n    88\tdetaches every outstanding request: the driver prints\n    89\t`;; remote request N lost: …` rather than holding the request against a\n    90\tdeadline, because the layer has no clock. The served stream is untouched by a\n    91\tdetach; typing `(connect …)` with the same URL reattaches through the same\n    92\tclient medium — the deposit medium and its cursor survive the socket's death,\n    93\tand only the attachment id changes.\n    94\t\n    95\t**`stop!` ends the served stream.** A server shutdown closes the\n    96\tservice-lifetime stream, and a connected client observes the ended stream —\n    97\t`The stream served at … ended` — which is terminal: there is nothing to\n    98\treattach to. An ordinary socket death without the stream ending reports a\n    99\tdetach instead, which does reattach.\n   100\t\n   101\t## Consequences of running on `yin.vm`\n   102\t\n   103\tThese follow from the VM plan's divergence register:\n   104\t\n   105\t- **User-defined macros evaluate correctly.** The REPL is wired through the `yin.vm.macro` stream topology. `yang.clojure` translates macros into native function calls which are intercepted by the expander on `program-in` before reaching the evaluator.\n   106\t- **`stream/take!` is gone.** Programs use `cursor` and `next!` on the\n   107\t  `stream` module, which this REPL registers.\n   108\t- **Park-on-full backpressure is absent under this composition.** The media\n   109\t  this REPL supplies are ring buffers that evict rather than answering `full`,\n   110\t  so writers never park. The VM itself remains total over `full`.\n   111\t\n   112\t## Server behavior\n   113\t\n   114\t`--port` serves one shared shell: every connected client\n   115\tevaluates against one serially threaded REPL state, and two clients each get\n   116\ttheir own answers. That shell is the server's own local prompt's shell — the\n   117\tone step owner threads the same value through both, so a definition typed at\n   118\tthe server's `yin>` prompt answers a remote request in the same tick, and a\n   119\tdefinition a remote client makes is visible at the local prompt on the next\n   120\ttick. The endpoint evaluates locally or reports that it does not proxy.\n   121\t`--host 127.0.0.1` remains the default boundary.\n   122\t\n   123\t## Publishing and loading code over the DHT\n   124\t\n   125\tWith `--index-store dht:<dir>` the code index is the durable, exclusively\n   126\tlocked directory store with a `dao.space.dht` node composed over it: the\n   127\tnode joins the DHT, shares the store's content when told to, and fetches\n   128\twhat a `require` asks for and the node does not hold. The `--dht-*` flags\n   129\tare refused with any other store.\n   130\t\n   131\t**The flags.** `--dht-peer host:port` (repeatable) names the bootstrap\n   132\tcontacts; the host must be an IP literal, and no peer means a solo node\n   133\tthat opens no socket, whose publications stay durable in the directory\n   134\tand acknowledged by no one. `--dht-publish` shares everything the\n   135\tdirectory holds with any peer that asks for an address; without it the\n   136\tnode fetches only. `--dht-bind ip` and `--dht-port p` set the socket's\n   137\taddress, loopback and ephemeral unless given, and need a peer;\n   138\t`--dht-max-inbound-bytes n` bounds the payload the node accepts from\n   139\tpeers, 64 MiB by default. `--dht-manifest :segment/...`, with or without\n   140\tits leading colon, hands the node a remote index manifest to hydrate\n   141\tbefore the first evaluation, and needs a peer too. `--dht-key file` loads\n   142\tthe publisher's stable Ed25519 key file, `--dht-keygen file` writes one\n   143\tand exits, and `--dht-principal hex` (repeatable, 64 lowercase\n   144\thexadecimal digits) declares a publisher whose signed names this node\n   145\thonors. A bind that fails, or a hydration that cannot complete, refuses\n   146\tthe shell with exit status 1 instead of composing over an empty index.\n   147\t\n   148\t```bash\n   149\tclj -M:clj-yin-repl --index-store dht:/tmp/yin-a --dht-peer 127.0.0.1:4001 \\\n   150\t    --dht-publish --dht-key /tmp/yin-a.key\n   151\t```\n   152\t\n   153\tThe banner states, before the node steps once and so before anything is\n   154\tshared, what will be shared: that publishing is on and everything in the\n   155\tdirectory's content path will be shared, or that the node fetches only.\n   156\tWith `--dht-key` it also names the principal that signs every name\n   157\tpublished here (`dht: names published here are signed by principal\n   158\ted25519:... (key from <file>)`); without one, that `(yin.link/publish ...)`\n   159\tis refused while names are still read, resolved, loaded and linked. The\n   160\tnode's first ticks print where the socket bound (`dht: node ...\n   161\tlistening on 127.0.0.1:53812; peers: ...; publishing`, or `fetch-only`).\n   162\t\n   163\t**Publishing a module by signed name.** `(require (quote yin.link))`\n   164\tinstalls the host module, and then `(yin.link/publish (quote my.lib)\n   165\t(quote [f]))` derives the module from this session's indexed code: the\n   166\tdefining programs of the exports and, to a fixed point, of the free names\n   167\tthey read, minted into all four code formats under one schema-1 manifest\n   168\tand materialized into the index store content-addressed. The answer names\n   169\tthe module, its manifest address, and the linker's own verdict per\n   170\tformat:\n   171\t\n   172\t```clojure\n   173\tyin> (yin.link/publish (quote my.lib) (quote [f]))\n   174\t{:module 'my.lib, :address :segment/..., :links {...}}\n   175\t```\n   176\t\n   177\tThe name binding is a set of Ed25519-signed envelopes binding the name to\n   178\tthat manifest address, committed through the session's indexer as one\n   179\ttransaction whose HEAD write announces the publication. The node then\n   180\treports the result: `dht: published :segment/... (N blobs)` followed by\n   181\t`acknowledged: sent to N peers`, or a PARTIAL or NOT acknowledged line\n   182\tnaming the count of blobs not sent, the first reason, and whether it is\n   183\tretrying; a publication that is not acknowledged is retried in the\n   184\tbackground while the node is open. Republishing a name at another\n   185\tmanifest retracts the standing assertion first. `:links` is per format,\n   186\tand a format it refused at publish time (an export reading a module-level\n   187\tdefinition from inside a lambda refuses the tree format) refuses at\n   188\trequire time too.\n   189\t\n   190\t**Loading it in another yin.repl.** Start the reader against a peer, with\n   191\tthe publisher's index manifest and its principal:\n   192\t\n   193\t```bash\n   194\tclj -M:clj-yin-repl --index-store dht:/tmp/yin-b --dht-peer 127.0.0.1:53812 \\\n   195\t    --dht-manifest :segment/... --dht-principal ed25519:...\n   196\t```\n   197\t\n   198\tHydration runs before the first evaluation: typed lines wait in the input\n   199\tmedium until the reader prints `dht: hydrated :<manifest>`, with the\n   200\tdatom and blob counts, and `evaluation admitted`. Then require by name:\n   201\t\n   202\t```clojure\n   203\tyin> (require (quote my.lib))\n   204\t;; require pending: my.lib (link N); lines typed meanwhile ...\n   205\tyin> 'my.lib\n   206\tyin> (my.lib/f 1)\n   207\t4201\n   208\t```\n   209\t\n   210\tA require whose closure the node does not hold prints the\n   211\t`;; require pending: ...` line and parks: the node fetches the module's\n   212\tclosure from the peers, the parked require is re-checked when the load\n   213\tends, lines typed meanwhile run when it completes, and `(abandon)` gives\n   214\tit up. The require answers the module's name and the export evaluates.\n   215\tEach VM's session links the module itself, so after `(vm :type)` require\n   216\tthe name again: the same published name evaluates on all four VMs.\n   217\t`(yin.link/names)` answers the names this node resolves, each with its\n   218\tmanifest address and the principal that asserted it.\n   219\t\n   220\t**Why a required name can be trusted.** Content is content-addressed,\n   221\tand every hop re-verifies it: a blob fetched from a peer is checked\n   222\tagainst the address it was asked for before it enters the local store,\n   223\tand the closure walk re-checks the manifest, every tree row, every\n   224\tderivation record and every image against its own address. A manifest\n   225\tlinks at a name only if it declares that name. Names resolve only from\n   226\tsigned assertions of declared principals: an undeclared publisher, or a\n   227\tproof that does not verify, refuses the require `:absent` with the\n   228\treasons as data. The same address asserted by several declared\n   229\tprincipals resolves with every asserter named; two distinct addresses\n   230\trefuse `:ambiguous-name`. A failed load is refused and forgotten, so a\n   231\tlater require starts a new load. The link source reads only the node's\n   232\town store, and the directory's lock keeps one owner per directory. A\n   233\tretraction whose target no loaded snapshot holds is reported once,\n   234\tglobally, by `(yin.link/names)`, never as a per-name diagnostic.\n   235\t`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md` is the contract for all of it.\n   236\t\n   237\t**The plain-Clojure path.** A non-REPL client takes the same path through\n   238\tplain functions: `dao.space.dht/join` over a store, `load-index` for a\n   239\tmanifest address (step the node with `dao.space.dht/step`; `load-status`\n   240\tanswers `:loading`, `:loaded`, or `:failed` with the reason as data),\n   241\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/names` to resolve names under an authority of\n   242\tdeclared principals (`yin.vm.linker.dht/authority`),\n   243\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/load-module` for a module's closure, and\n   244\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/link` for one format's image; every failure answers\n   245\tthe data of that design's section 9. At the prompt,\n   246\t`(require (quote dao.space.dht))` exposes the same calls as host\n   247\tfunctions over the shell's own node.\n   248\t\n   249\t**Operational notes.** Keep the key file: a lost key can never sign\n   250\tagain, and a new key is a new principal. Receivers verify names against\n   251\tthe publisher's principal, not against where the bytes came from, so\n   252\thand the principal out with the network address and replace the\n   253\tdeclaration when a key rotates. The Node reader the process tests spawn\n   254\tis a build artifact: `bb build:yin-repl-node` writes `target/yin-repl.js`,\n   255\tand a missing build fails `test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`. A fresh\n   256\tworktree must run `clj -M:antlr-gen` before JVM test runs: the generated\n   257\tparser path, `build/antlr/python3/classes`, is on the classpath and\n   258\tabsent until generated.\n   259\t\n   260\tSee [`docs/design/yin.repl.implementation-plan.md`](../../../../docs/design/yin.repl.implementation-plan.md)\n   261\tfor the plan this REPL implements and the contract documents it is subordinate\n   262\tto.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
